\begin{spacing}{1.0}
\noindent\small
The v7.03 generation of the \macrosurf{} library shipped a three-part omega-gated exit cascade that was \emph{mechanism-validated}---it fired end-to-end and capped a losing trade at $-0.83\%$---but never \emph{edge-validated}, because every macro instrument on the venue serves only three to four days of history. The binding constraint on the library is therefore not mechanism quality but \emph{validation capacity}. We argue that the correct next evolution is not a bundle of crypto-specific model and feature tweaks (which would mutate every compositional interface at once and forfeit attributability) but a single, disciplined change of \emph{substrate}.

We make three contributions. First, we promote the substrate to a first-class library interface $\Vset$, extending the compositional system $\mathcal{T}=(\Mset,\Fset,\Eset,\Xset,\Pset)$ with an explicit validation seam, and we define a \textbf{data-sufficiency precondition} that gates any mutation of the model, features, entry, or exit behind a certification that the substrate carries enough continuous history to validate it. This makes the near-miss of the v7.03 equity-index leg---a strategy that was almost shipped on three days of data---structurally impossible. Second, \cryptosurf{} v7.04 executes a \textbf{pure portfolio mutation} ($\Pset$ only): the universe reverts to the liquid crypto leg (BTC, ETH), while the SVR-RBF model, the surf-kinematics feature manifold, the omega-gated entry generator, and the entire v7.03 exit cascade are frozen \emph{verbatim}. The deep, continuous one-minute crypto history then yields the first \emph{attributable edge measurement} of the omega-exit stack, with per-exit-reason profit attribution published through the existing \hermes{} atomic-snapshot contract so the library observes its own falsification. We report a \emph{preliminary} execution of this loop over two out-of-sample folds ($n=64$ trades): the \texttt{omega\_reversal} bound carries the mass of exits and bleeds while the patient \texttt{roi} holds are the only positive bucket---directionally consistent with the v7.03 duration thesis---but we hold the edge verdict, as the sample is far too small to certify it. Third, we specify the v7.05 roadmap: \textbf{restoring reinforcement learning} (\texttt{MutantReinforcementLearner}) at the model interface. We show that its five-action environment's reward function already encodes, \emph{declaratively}, the very duration-and-left-tail discipline that v7.03 hand-coded imperatively---and that it was shelved not for being wrong but for want of a substrate deep enough to train it. v7.04 is precisely the act of building that substrate.
\end{spacing}

