\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

\subsection{From Composition to Correction}

The v7.02 generation \citep{mutantdefi2026hypersurf} established a compositional macro-futures architecture: a support-vector regression (SVR-RBF) omega predictor, a four-dimensional surf-kinematics feature manifold, a chop-regime classifier, and competitive entry selection, deployed as a fleet of single-asset strategies over Hyperliquid perpetuals spanning crypto, energy, and metals. That paper closed by enumerating eight \emph{identified opportunities for v7.03}. The present work does not attempt all eight. It executes the one the live telemetry proved most urgent---closing the \textbf{stoploss-parameter utilization gap}---and pairs it with the structurally adjacent fixes that the same telemetry demanded.

The discipline here is deliberate. A trading system that mutates many components at once cannot attribute a change in performance to any one of them. v7.03 is therefore scoped as a \emph{single evolutionary step}: the model, the targets, and the entry logic are frozen; only the exit architecture and the tradeable universe are altered. This preserves the compositional interfaces of \citet{mutantdefi2026hypersurf} (\cref{def:single-step}) and yields an attributable experiment.

\subsection{The Diagnosed Pathology: Payoff Asymmetry}

Between June 3 and June 12, 2026 the v7.02 fleet ran in dry-run forward-walk. The aggregate signal hit-rate was high, yet the metals legs were net-negative. The cause was not signal quality but \emph{payoff geometry}: exits consisted only of a shallow ROI ladder (capping wins near $+0.6\%$, decaying to $+0.2\%$) and a single flat $-1.2\%$ hard stop, with \texttt{use\_exit\_signal} disabled and no custom stoploss. The consequence was a winner whose magnitude was capped by design and a loser whose magnitude was bounded only by a flat stop that, in practice, realized worse than its nominal level and was reached only after long bleeds.

