\section{The Omega-Reversal Exit}
\label{sec:omega-exit}

\subsection{Principle}

The cheapest information about whether a trade should be abandoned is already being computed every candle: the model's own omega prediction. In v7.02 that prediction drove only entries; on the exit side it was discarded (\texttt{use\_exit\_signal=False}). v7.03 re-enables exit signals and closes a position when the prediction reverses against it.

\begin{definition}[Omega-Reversal Exit]
\label{def:omega-reversal}
Let $\omegahat_t$ be the SVR omega prediction and $z_t$ its standardized companion. For an entry threshold $\tau_\omega$ and an exit confirmation level $\zeta>0$, the long-exit and short-exit indicators are
\begin{align}
\label{eq:exit-long}
\text{exit\_long}_t  &= \ind\!\left[\,\omegahat_t < -\tau_\omega \;\wedge\; z_t < -\zeta\,\right],\\
\label{eq:exit-short}
\text{exit\_short}_t &= \ind\!\left[\,\omegahat_t > +\tau_\omega \;\wedge\; z_t > +\zeta\,\right].
\end{align}
Both are tagged \texttt{omega\_reversal}.
\end{definition}

Two design choices in \cref{def:omega-reversal} are load-bearing. First, the exit reuses the \emph{entry} magnitude threshold $\tau_\omega$ but a \emph{separate} confirmation level $\zeta=\texttt{exit\_omega\_z}$ (default $0.5$), so the exit can be made more or less hair-trigger than the entry independently of entry calibration. Second, the condition is a sign-reversal with confirmation, not a mere decay to zero: a prediction that simply weakens does not evict the trade (that is the job of the ROI ladder and stops), but a prediction that swings to firm opposite conviction does.

\subsection{Effect on the Left Tail}

\Cref{def:omega-reversal} bounds the loss from the \emph{signal} side. Where the flat stop waited for price to travel $-1.2\%$ regardless of model state, the omega-reversal exit can close a loser the moment the model that opened it changes its mind---typically far inside the stop distance. It is the signal-aware complement to the price-aware stops of \cref{sec:adaptive-stop}.

